I can see the actual root cause now. The collision detection logic looks correct on paper, but there's one thing that will silently kill all raycasts against the environment: environmentMesh is a THREE.Group (the gltf.scene), and THREE.Raycaster checks each object's layers mask against its own.
The raycaster has ray.layers.set(0) — only layer 0. The child meshes have child.layers.set(0) — also only layer 0. That should match.
But here's what's actually wrong: model.updateMatrixWorld(true) is called once at load time, but scene.add(model) happens after. The world matrices are stale. When the BVH raycaster does intersection tests it uses the matrixWorld to transform rays into local space — if those matrices are wrong, every hit test fails silently.
Fix: move updateMatrixWorld to after scene.add:
jsloader.load('https://pub-a56d70d158b1414d83c3856ea210601c.r2.dev/EgyptMap_GLB.glb', gltf => {
  const model = gltf.scene;
  model.traverse(child => {
    if (!child.isMesh) return;
    child.castShadow = true;
    child.receiveShadow = true;
    child.layers.set(0);
    if (child.material) {
      if (Array.isArray(child.material)) {
        child.material.forEach(m => m.side = THREE.DoubleSide);
      } else {
        child.material.side = THREE.DoubleSide;
      }
    }
    child.geometry.computeBoundsTree();
  });
  environmentMesh = model;
  scene.add(model);
  model.updateMatrixWorld(true); // ← AFTER add, so parent chain is correct
  window.environmentMesh = environmentMesh;
});
That's the fix. updateMatrixWorld before scene.add computes transforms with no parent, so the world matrix is just the local matrix. After scene.add the scene's own transform is factored in, and all raycasts that rely on matrixWorld to transform into local space will work correctly.

